{"bill":{"id":"119-s-1833","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"1833","title":"Leadership in CET Act","introducedDate":"2025-05-21","latestActionDate":"2025-05-21","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Commerce","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"B001243","cosponsorBioguideIds":["W000800","B001319","S001150","C001088"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Leadership in Critical and Emerging Technologies Act or the Leadership in CET Act This bill directs the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO) to establish and carry out a pilot program to expedite the patent examination process for patents involving critical or emerging technologies (i.e., artificial intelligence, semiconductor design, or quantum information science). The USPTO must report to Congress on the impact and effectiveness of the pilot program.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/1833","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s1833.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:01.821Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-1833","plainSummary":"This bill directs the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office to create a pilot program that speeds up patent reviews for inventions in critical and emerging technology areas, specifically artificial intelligence, semiconductor design, and quantum information science. The office would be required to report to Congress on how well the program works. The goal is to reduce waiting times for patents in these strategic technology fields.","industries":[{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill explicitly targets patent expediting for AI, semiconductor design, and quantum computing, all core technology-sector activities."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Patent law firms and intellectual-property practitioners would handle the expedited applications and navigate the new pil